W("Reference to context: 'She was one of those pretty, young ladies, born as if through an error of destiny, into a family of clerks.' (i) Who is she? (ii) What 'error of destiny' does she feel? (iii) What does this show about her?", 3, "(i) Matilda Loisel. (1) (ii) That she was born pretty and refined in a poor family without dowry or means of marrying a rich man. (1) (iii) She is vain, discontented and has an unrealistic idea of her place in life. (1)", "M"),
W("Reference to context: 'When she seated herself for dinner opposite her husband who uncovered the tureen with a delighted air...' (i) What does the husband say? (ii) What does Matilda think? (iii) What contrast is shown?", 3, "(i) 'Oh! the good potpie! I know nothing better than that.' (1) (ii) She thinks of elegant dinners, shining silver and exquisite food in marvellous dishes. (1) (iii) The contented simple husband versus the dissatisfied wife who longs for luxury. (1)", "M"),
W("Reference to context: 'He turned a little pale, for he had saved just this sum to buy a gun.' (i) Who? (ii) Why pale? (iii) What does his response show?", 3, "(i) Loisel. (1) (ii) Because Matilda asked for exactly the amount he had saved for his gun. (1) (iii) He is generous and unselfish, giving up his own pleasure for his wife's happiness. (1)", "M"),
