# ---------------- HARD ----------------
W("Extract-based: 'Instead of being delighted, as her husband had hoped, she threw the invitation spitefully upon the table murmuring, \"What do you suppose I want with that?\"' (i) What did Loisel expect? (ii) What does 'spitefully' reveal? (iii) What is the irony here? (iv) How does Loisel respond?", 4, "(i) That the card would make his wife happy. (1) (ii) Her ill-temper and discontent. (1) (iii) A rare chance to enter high society is thrown away, though she always longs for it. (1) (iv) He explains it is an occasion and fine, then offers to buy a suitable dress. (1)", "H"),
W("Extract-based: 'She looked at him with an irritated eye and declared impatiently, \"What do you suppose I have to wear to such a thing as that?\"' (i) What problem does she raise? (ii) How does Loisel react? (iii) What does the incident show about their relationship? (iv) What does she finally ask?", 4, "(i) That she has no suitable dress. (1) (ii) He stammers and suggests her theatre dress; then he is dismayed to see her weeping. (1) (iii) He is patient and loving, she is demanding and self-centred. (1) (iv) Four hundred francs for a costume. (1)", "H"),
W("'The course of the Loisels' life changed due to the necklace.' Comment in about 100 words.", 5, "Before the loss the Loisels lived modestly, though Matilda was discontented. The lost necklace turned their small trouble into a disaster: they spent the inheritance and borrowed money from usurers to replace it. For ten years Matilda worked as a servant, and Loisel took extra jobs. The beautiful, proud woman became old and hard, and the contented husband bore heavy work. The tragedy is made worse by the final revelation that the necklace was false. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("'What was the cause of Matilda's ruin? How could she have avoided it?' Answer in about 100 words.", 5, "The cause was her vanity and love of show: she wanted jewels for the ball and borrowed a diamond necklace, then did not tell her friend about its loss. She could have worn natural flowers as her husband suggested, or been content with her simple dress. Above all, she should have told Mme Forestier the truth at once; her friend might have said that the necklace was false and saved ten years of misery. Honesty would have prevented ruin. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("'What would have happened to Matilda if she had confessed to her friend that she had lost her necklace?' Answer in about 80 words.", 4, "Mme Forestier, who is kind (she lent the necklace freely), would probably have forgiven her and told her that the jewels were false and worth no more than five hundred francs. The Loisels would have avoided debt and ten years of hardship, and their friendship would have remained. (4)", "H"),
W("'If you were caught in a situation like this, how would you have dealt with it?' Give your reasoned answer in about 100 words.", 5, "I would have informed my friend at once and honestly, apologised, and offered to pay or replace it in a sensible way after learning its true value. I would have checked the value before taking any loan. I would not have borrowed an expensive item to show off. An honest explanation saves money, time and friendship, and kind people usually forgive a sincere mistake. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("The characters speak in English in the story. Do you think this is their language? Give clues from the text.", 4, "No. The story is set in Paris, with French names and titles (M., Mme, Monsieur, Madame), francs and sous as money, and places such as the Palais-Royal and Champs-Elysees. The characters would speak French; the text is a translation into English. (4)", "H"),
W("'Honesty is the best policy' - discuss in relation to the story in about 100 words.", 5, "Matilda's dishonesty (hiding the loss) cost her ten years. Had she been honest, the truth that the necklace was false would have come out at once. In life, concealing a mistake usually makes it worse, while an honest confession earns understanding. Honesty keeps friendships and peace of mind, though it needs courage. The story proves the proverb in a painful way. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("'We should be content with what life gives us.' Discuss with reference to Matilda and Loisel (about 80 words).", 4, "Loisel was happy with potpie and a quiet life, while Matilda longed for luxuries and was always unhappy. Her discontent led to her wish to look rich at the party and to the loss that ruined them. Contentment means being grateful for what one has while still working to improve it honestly. Ambition is good, but not vanity or envy. (4)", "H"),
W("Case study: Rohit, a Class 10 student in Jaipur, borrows his cousin's expensive watch for a function and loses it. He is afraid to tell. Using the lesson, advise him with three points.", 4, "Tell his cousin and parents the truth immediately; offer to pay or help find or replace it honestly, and check its real value first. Recognise that hiding a mistake, as Matilda did, only increases the cost. (4)", "H", wp=True),
W("Why is the title 'The Necklace' apt? Give four points.", 4, "The necklace is central: Matilda borrows it, loses it, and replaces it; it causes the debt, the change in life and the final shock; it is a symbol of false appearance and vanity, because it is worthless but looks valuable. (4)", "H"),
W("Do you think the ending is effective? Explain the irony in about 80 words.", 4, "The ending is powerful. The reader is told that Matilda smiles with 'proud and simple joy' at having repaid the debt, then learns that the necklace was false. The ten years of sacrifice were for nothing. The irony lies in a woman who valued appearances being ruined by a false jewel. A sudden twist leaves the reader shocked and thoughtful. (4)", "H"),
W("Write a diary entry (about 100 words) by Matilda on the night she discovered the necklace was lost.", 5, "Model: It is nearly dawn and I am sick with fear. I was the prettiest at the ball, but when I took off my wraps the necklace was gone. We looked everywhere, in my dress, my cloak, the cab, but found nothing. Loisel has gone out on foot to search. How can I face Mme Forestier? She trusted me. He says we must write that the clasp is broken, to gain time. I am too ashamed to go to bed. Why did I want to look rich? (5; content 2, expression 2, format 1)", "H"),
W("Write a letter (about 100 words) from Matilda to Mme Forestier, written years earlier, honestly confessing the loss of the necklace and asking for her forgiveness.", 5, "Model: Dear Jeanne, I have something painful to tell you. On the night of the ball I lost the diamond necklace you kindly lent me. We searched everywhere, went to the police and put advertisements in the papers, but it could not be found. Instead of telling you, I was afraid and wrote that the clasp was broken. I am deeply sorry. Please tell me its value; I shall repay you however long it takes. I hope you will forgive me. Your friend, Matilda. (5; content 2, expression 2, format 1)", "H"),
W("Write a short dialogue (about 80 words) between Matilda and Mme Forestier in which Matilda tells the truth at the start, so that the story ends differently.", 4, "Model: Matilda: Jeanne, I must confess something. I lost your necklace at the ball. Mme Forestier: Oh Matilda, do not weep. Those diamonds were false; they were worth only five hundred francs. Matilda: False! And we were ready to sell everything! Mme Forestier: Never mind; I am only glad you told me. Matilda: I shall never hide a mistake again. (4)", "H"),
W("Compare Matilda at the beginning and the end of the story in a table of four points (appearance, work, attitude, speech).", 4, "Beginning: young, pretty; no work, only dreams; discontented; refined. End: old, hands red; hard household labour; accepting and proud of paying off debt; loud tone. (1 per point)", "H"),
