 ("h2", "7. Extract explanations"),
 ("p", "**Extract 1:** 'She suffered incessantly, feeling herself born for all delicacies and luxuries.' Matilda's suffering is not from real poverty but from envy and pride. The author shows that unhappiness often comes from within."),
 ("p", "**Extract 2:** 'She danced with enthusiasm, intoxicated with pleasure, thinking of nothing but all this admiration.' This is the high point of her life. The word 'intoxicated' warns that her happiness is unreal and will not last."),
 ("p", "**Extract 3:** 'Oh! My poor Matilda! Mine were false.' The last line gives the twist: ten years of sacrifice were for a necklace of little value. It makes the reader feel both pity for Matilda and the lesson about honesty."),
 ("p", "**Extract 4:** 'She learned the odious work of a kitchen... And, clothed like a woman of the people, she went to the grocer's, the butcher's and the fruiterer's, with her basket on her arm.' The long list of chores shows the full cost of the loss. The author does not blame Matilda here; he praises her ('heroically') for facing the debt, and this makes readers sympathise with her despite her earlier vanity."),
